% Lesson 8a (docs/minicourses/minicourse-evaluation.org) - an axiom is
% exposed.  The three arguments of lesson9_evaluation.fspy and, after
% them, attackrule (the author's example, 2026-10-05): a refutation of
% A->B from a presumption of D, meant to attack the rule pro applies, the
% declared axiom r1.  Until 2026-10-16 the unfolder expanded sites only
% and attackrule took no part in pro's debate; since the horn rule
% (tasks.org, aida-rational-closure) r1 is an exposed node of pro, its
% statement A->B gets its debate inside pro, attackrule stands in that
% debate's attacker wing, and loses to the strict axiom in phase 1.
lk.
declare A,B,C:bool.
declare r1: (A -> B).
declare r2: (C -> ~A).
declare nc: (~C).

argument pro : (B).
cut (A -> B) th.
axiom r1.
elim.
by default.
next.
axiom.
dixi.

counterargument con : (A).
cut (~A) x.
cut (C -> ~A) th.
axiom r2.
elim.
by default.
next.
axiom.
elim.
axiom.
dixi.

counterargument killC : (C).
cut (~C) x.
axiom nc.
elim.
axiom.
dixi.

declare D : bool.
declare r3 : (D-> ~(A->B)).
counterargument attackrule : (A->B).
cut (D-> ~(A->B)) th.
axiom r3.
elim.
by default.
next.
elim.
axiom.
dixi.

evaluate pro.
evaluate attackrule.
